<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Throughout this paper <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is a finite, undirected, loopless graph without
multiple edges, with vertex set <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(G)</annotation></semantics></math> of cardinality <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|V\left(G\right)\right|=n\left(G\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|V\left(G\right)\right|=n\left(G\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, and edge set <math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(G)</annotation></semantics></math> of size
<math id="S1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|E\left(G\right)\right|=m\left(G\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|E\left(G\right)\right|=m\left(G\right)</annotation></semantics></math>. An edge
<math id="S1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e\in E(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e\in E(G)</annotation></semantics></math> connecting vertices <math id="S1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> will also be denoted as <math id="S1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="xy" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">xy</annotation></semantics></math> or
<math id="S1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="yx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">yx</annotation></semantics></math>. In this case, <math id="S1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> are said to be <span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">adjacent</span>. An
<span id="S1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">independent set</span> in <math id="S1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is a set of pairwise non-adjacent vertices. A
<span id="S1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">maximal independent set</span> of <math id="S1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is the one that cannot be extended
further. A largest independent set in a graph is called a <span id="S1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">maximum
independent set</span>, and its cardinality is denoted by <math id="S1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha(G)</annotation></semantics></math>, known as the
<span id="S1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">independence number</span> of <math id="S1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. It is well known that <math id="S1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha(G)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha(G)=1</annotation></semantics></math> if
and only if <math id="S1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is a complete graph.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> be a subset of <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(G)</annotation></semantics></math>. We denote <math id="S1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G[A]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G[A]</annotation></semantics></math> the induced subgraph of <math id="S1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> on
<math id="S1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, while by <math id="S1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G-A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G-A</annotation></semantics></math> we mean <math id="S1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G[V(G)-A]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G[V(G)-A]</annotation></semantics></math>. The neighborhood of <math id="S1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is the
set</p>
