<p id="S1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">the closed neighborhood of <math id="S1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{G}[A]=A\cup N_{G}(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{G}[A]=A\cup N_{G}(A)</annotation></semantics></math>, and the
<span id="S1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">localization</span> of <math id="S1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to <math id="S1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{A}=G-N_{G}[A]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{A}=G-N_{G}[A]</annotation></semantics></math>. If
<math id="S1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=\{v\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=\{v\}</annotation></semantics></math>, we write <math id="S1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{G}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{G}(v)</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. <math id="S1.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{G}[v]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{G}[v]</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G-v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G-v</annotation></semantics></math>) instead of
<math id="S1.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{G}(\{v\})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{G}(\{v\})</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. <math id="S1.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{G}[\{v\}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{G}[\{v\}]</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{\{v\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{\{v\}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.p2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G-\{v\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G-\{v\}</annotation></semantics></math>). The number
<math id="S1.p2.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg_{G}(v)=\left|N_{G}(v)\right|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>deg</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg_{G}(v)=\left|N_{G}(v)\right|</annotation></semantics></math> is called the <span id="S1.p2.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">degree</span> of
<math id="S1.p2.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S1.p2.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. A vertex of degree zero is an <span id="S1.p2.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">isolated vertex</span>. In
addition, <math id="S1.p2.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta(G)</annotation></semantics></math> is the minimum degree of vertices of <math id="S1.p2.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">A graph is called <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">well-covered</span> if all its maximal independent sets
have the same size <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>, <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>. For instance, all complete graph
on <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> vertices, denoted by <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, is well-covered and the only cycles that
are well-covered are <math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{3},C_{4},C_{5}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>5</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{3},C_{4},C_{5},</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{7}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{7}</annotation></semantics></math>. Additionally, the path
on <math id="S1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> vertices, denoted by <math id="S1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, is well-covered if and only if <math id="S1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=1,2,4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=1,2,4</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we extend the exploration of well-covered graphs. In 1975,
Staples introduced the hereditary <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> as a generalization of
well-covered graphs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>, <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>. For a positive integer <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>,
a graph <math id="S1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> belongs to class <math id="S1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n(G)\geq p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n(G)\geq p</annotation></semantics></math> and for every
<math id="S1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> pairwise disjoint independent sets <math id="S1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{1},\ldots,A_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{1},\ldots,A_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> there exist <math id="S1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>
pairwise disjoint maximum independent sets <math id="S1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{1},\ldots,S_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{1},\ldots,S_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> such
that <math id="S1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}\subseteq S_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}\subseteq S_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S1.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq p</annotation></semantics></math>. The graph <math id="S1.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\in\mathbf{W}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\in\mathbf{W}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> is also called the <math id="S1.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> graph</span>. Observe, by
definition, that a graph is <math id="S1.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if it is
well-covered, and</p>
