<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{1}\supseteq\mathbf{W}_{2}\supseteq\mathbf{W}_{3}\supseteq\cdots\supseteq\mathbf{W}_{p}\supseteq\cdots." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊇</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⊇</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">⊇</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">⊇</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">⊇</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{1}\supseteq\mathbf{W}_{2}\supseteq\mathbf{W}_{3}\supseteq\cdots\supseteq\mathbf{W}_{p}\supseteq\cdots.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">Various methods for constructing <math id="S1.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> graphs are presented in
details in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>, <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">A well-covered graph (with at least two vertices) is <math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-<span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">well-covered</span>
if the deletion of any vertex of the graph leaves it well-covered
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>. For instance, <math id="S1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-well-covered, while a path on
four vertices <math id="S1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> is well-covered, but not <math id="S1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-well-covered. Furthermore,
the close relationship between <math id="S1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> graphs and <math id="S1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-well-covered
graphs is demonstrated as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.1</span></span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">[</span><a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>, <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">]</span></cite><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> Let <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a graph without isolated
vertices. Then <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-well-covered if and only if <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ G</annotation></semantics></math> is a
<math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> graph.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The <math id="S1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> graphs exhibit numerous intriguing properties and
characteristics, both in combinatorics and commutative algebra, as detailed in
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>, <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>, <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>. To illustrate, consider the polynomial
ring <math id="S1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R=K[x_{1},\ldots,x_{n}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=K[x_{1},\ldots,x_{n}]</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> variables over a field <math id="S1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>
be a graph with vertex set <math id="S1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V=\{1,\ldots,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V=\{1,\ldots,n\}</annotation></semantics></math>. We associate to the graph <math id="S1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> a
quadratic square-free monomial ideal</p>
<table id="S1.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(G)=(x_{i}x_{j}\mid ij\in E(G))\subseteq R," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(G)=(x_{i}x_{j}\mid ij\in E(G))\subseteq R,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p6.2" class="ltx_p">which is called the <span id="S1.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">edge ideal</span> of <math id="S1.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. We say that <math id="S1.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is
<span id="S1.p6.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Cohen–Macaulay</span> (resp. <span id="S1.p6.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Gorenstein</span>) if <math id="S1.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(G)</annotation></semantics></math> is a
Cohen–Macaulay (resp. Gorenstein) ideal. Notably, every Gorenstein graph is
Cohen–Macaulay, while the converse is not generally true.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">It has been established that <math id="S1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is well-covered whenever it is
Cohen–Macaulay <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>, Proposition 6.1.21]</cite>, and <math id="S1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> belongs to
<math id="S1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> whenever it is Gorenstein <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, Lemma 2.5]</cite>. For
triangle-free graphs, <math id="S1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> graphs are also Gorenstein
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, Theorem 4.4]</cite>. However, in general, not all <math id="S1.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> graphs
are Gorenstein. An example of a <math id="S1.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> graph that is always
Cohen–Macaulay is the clique corona graph. Let <math id="S1.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}=\{H_{v}:v\in V(G)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}=\{H_{v}:v\in V(G)\}</annotation></semantics></math> be a family of non-empty graphs indexed by the vertex set of a graph
<math id="S1.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. The <span id="S1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">corona</span> <math id="S1.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\circ\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\circ\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S1.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> is
defined as the disjoint union of <math id="S1.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> for each <math id="S1.p7.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V(G)</annotation></semantics></math>, with
additional edges connecting each vertex <math id="S1.p7.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V(G)</annotation></semantics></math> to all the vertices of
<math id="S1.p7.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>. When all graphs <math id="S1.p7.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S1.p7.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> are
complete graphs, <math id="S1.p7.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\circ\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\circ\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> is referred to as a <span id="S1.p7.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">clique
corona graph</span>. A clique corona graph is not only a well-covered graph
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>, Theorem 1]</cite>, but also a Cohen–Macaulay graph <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, Theorem
2.6]</cite>. If <math id="S1.p7.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{v}=K_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{v}=K_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> for every <math id="S1.p7.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V(G)</annotation></semantics></math>, we use <math id="S1.p7.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\circ K_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\circ K_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> to
denote <math id="S1.p7.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\circ\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\circ\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1.2</span></span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">[</span><a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">]</span></cite><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> For <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, a graph <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>-quasi-regularizable
if</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\cdot\left|S\right|\leq|N_{G}(S)|," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\cdot\left|S\right|\leq|N_{G}(S)|,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for every independent set <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=1</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is said to be a <span id="S1.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">quasi-regularizable</span> graph
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.3</span></span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">[</span><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a><span id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">]</span></cite><span id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> Every well-covered graph without isolated vertices
is quasi-regularizable.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">It is noteworthy pointing out that the structure of <math id="S1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>-quasi-regularizable graph implies the correlation between the number of
vertices with the independence number. In particular, by the theorem above,
one obtains that <math id="S1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\left(G\right)\geq 2\alpha\left(G\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\left(G\right)\geq 2\alpha\left(G\right)</annotation></semantics></math> holds for
every well-covered graph <math id="S1.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore, if <math id="S1.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S1.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> graph
with <math id="S1.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, then it is <math id="S1.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p-1)</annotation></semantics></math>-quasi-regularizable <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, Theorem 2.6
(iii)]</cite>. We have also conjectured that <math id="S1.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S1.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>-quasi-regularizable
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, Conjecture 2.7]</cite>. However, it turned out that this assertion does
not hold in general. For example, <math id="S1.p9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{5}\in\mathbf{W}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{5}\in\mathbf{W}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, but it is not
<math id="S1.p9.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-quasi-regularizable. Nonetheless, in Section <a href="#S2" title="2 Quasi-regularizability of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs ‣ Log-concavity of the independence polynomials of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, we confirm the following.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.8</span> <span id="S1.p10.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a connected <math id="S1.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> graph, where <math id="S1.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\neq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\neq 2</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S1.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S1.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>-quasi-regularizable if and only if <math id="S1.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n(G)\geq(p+1)\cdot\alpha(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n(G)\geq(p+1)\cdot\alpha(G)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Beyond exploring graph structures, graph theory presents numerous intriguing
problems related to log-concavity, a concept that has deep implications in
combinatorics and algebra <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>. For instance, it is well-known that the
matching polynomial of a graph has only real zeros, making it log-concave
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>. This property is significant for understanding graph matchings
and their applications in network theory. Recently, a major breakthrough was
achieved with the resolution of the log-concavity of the chromatic polynomial
of a graph <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>, which has important implications for graph coloring
and phase transitions in statistical physics. Another key polynomial
associated with a graph is the <span id="S1.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">independence polynomial</span> of a graph
<math id="S1.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted <math id="S1.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(G;x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(G;x)</annotation></semantics></math>, which is defined in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> as follows:</p>
<table id="S1.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(G;x)=\sum_{k=0}^{\alpha(G)}s_{k}x^{k}=s_{0}+s_{1}x+\cdots+s_{\alpha(G)}x^{\alpha(G)}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(G;x)=\sum_{k=0}^{\alpha(G)}s_{k}x^{k}=s_{0}+s_{1}x+\cdots+s_{\alpha(G)}x^{\alpha(G)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p11.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S1.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the number of independent sets of cardinality <math id="S1.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> in
the graph <math id="S1.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. The independence polynomial <math id="S1.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(G;x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(G;x)</annotation></semantics></math> is said to be:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p12" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">log-concave</span> if <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{k}^{2}\geq s_{k-1}\cdot s_{k+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{k}^{2}\geq s_{k-1}\cdot s_{k+1}</annotation></semantics></math> for all
<math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq k\leq\alpha(G)-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq k\leq\alpha(G)-1</annotation></semantics></math>; or</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">unimodal</span> if there exists an index <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq k\leq\alpha(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq k\leq\alpha(G)</annotation></semantics></math> such
that</p>
<table id="S1.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{0}\leq\cdots\leq s_{k-1}\leq s_{k}\geq s_{k+1}\geq\cdots\geq s_{\alpha(G)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">≤</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">≤</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">≥</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">≥</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{0}\leq\cdots\leq s_{k-1}\leq s_{k}\geq s_{k+1}\geq\cdots\geq s_{\alpha(G)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S1.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p13.1" class="ltx_p">A well-known result by Chudnovsky and Seymour in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> states that all the
roots of <math id="S1.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(G;x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(G;x)</annotation></semantics></math> are real whenever <math id="S1.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is a claw-free graph, which also
implies the log-concavity of <math id="S1.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(G;x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(G;x)</annotation></semantics></math> for all claw-free graphs <math id="S1.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. The
study of the independence polynomial is a rich area with extensive literature,
including works addressing the log-concavity problems (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>, <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>, <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite> and their references).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 1.4</span></span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">[</span><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a><span id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">]</span></cite><span id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">  If <math id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is log-concave and <math id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is unimodal,
then <math id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(x)\cdot Q(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(x)\cdot Q(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is unimodal, while the product of two log-concave
polynomials is log-concave.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p14.1" class="ltx_p">In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>, Alavi, Malde, Schwenk, and Erdös proved that for any
permutation <math id="S1.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S1.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,2,\dots,\alpha(G)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,2,\dots,\alpha(G)\}</annotation></semantics></math>, there is a graph <math id="S1.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> such
that</p>
<table id="S1.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\pi(1)}&lt;s_{\pi(2)}&lt;\cdots&lt;s_{\pi(\alpha(G))}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">&lt;</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">&lt;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\pi(1)}&lt;s_{\pi(2)}&lt;\cdots&lt;s_{\pi(\alpha(G))}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S1.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p15.1" class="ltx_p">This result highlights the varied behaviors that graph polynomials can
display. Additionally, they conjectured that the independence polynomial
<math id="S1.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(G;x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(G;x)</annotation></semantics></math> is unimodal for any tree or forest <math id="S1.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. This conjecture remains
unresolved and continues to inspire ongoing research. Recently, it was
demonstrated that there are infinite families of trees whose independence
polynomials are not log-concave <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>. This finding challenges earlier
assumptions and suggests new directions for exploring the conditions under
which log-concavity and unimodality hold. It is also worth noting that the
independence polynomials of some well-covered graphs are not log-concave
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p16.1" class="ltx_p">Revisiting the open conjecture of the unimodality of independence polynomials
of trees stated in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>, it is known that <math id="S1.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(G;x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(G;x)</annotation></semantics></math> is log-concave
whenever <math id="S1.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is a well-covered spider <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>. Additionally, Radcliffe
verified that the independence polynomials of trees with up to <math id="S1.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="25" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>25</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">25</annotation></semantics></math> vertices
are log-concave <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>. Zhu and Chen, in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>, applied
factorization methods to show the log-concavity of independence polynomials in
some special cases of trees. In contrast, in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>, the authors
demonstrated that there exist exactly two trees of order <math id="S1.p16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="26" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>26</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">26</annotation></semantics></math> whose
independence polynomials are not log-concave.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p17.1" class="ltx_p">It is known that for every well-covered graph <math id="S1.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> whose <math id="S1.p17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{girth}(G)\geq 6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>girth</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{girth}(G)\geq 6</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\neq\{C_{7},K_{1}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\neq\{C_{7},K_{1}\}</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="S1.p17.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is well-covered if and only if <math id="S1.p17.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=H\circ K_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=H\circ K_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> for some graph <math id="S1.p17.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>. The conjecture about the
unimodality of <math id="S1.p17.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(G\circ K_{1};x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(G\circ K_{1};x)</annotation></semantics></math> for all graphs <math id="S1.p17.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> was stated in
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, Conjecture 3.3]</cite>, validated in the case <math id="S1.p17.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\left(G\right)\leq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\left(G\right)\leq 4</annotation></semantics></math> in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, Conjecture 3.3]</cite> and extended to <math id="S1.p17.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5\leq\alpha\left(G\right)\leq 8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5\leq\alpha\left(G\right)\leq 8</annotation></semantics></math> in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>. In addition, the unimodality of the
independence polynomial of clique corona graphs <math id="S1.p17.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\circ K_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\circ K_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> was
investigated for various classes of graphs such as: <math id="S1.p17.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is a claw-free graph
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, Corollary 3.12]</cite>; <math id="S1.p17.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is a quasi-regularizable graph with
<math id="S1.p17.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha(G)\leq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha(G)\leq 4</annotation></semantics></math>; <math id="S1.p17.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is an arbitrary graph and <math id="S1.p17.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying <math id="S1.p17.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p+1)(p+2)\geq n(G)+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p+1)(p+2)\geq n(G)+1</annotation></semantics></math>; and <math id="S1.p17.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is quasi-regularizable graph with <math id="S1.p17.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha(G)\leq p+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha(G)\leq p+1</annotation></semantics></math>
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>. As an application, it was proven that <math id="S1.p17.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(S_{n}\circ K_{p};x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(S_{n}\circ K_{p};x)</annotation></semantics></math> is
unimodal for all <math id="S1.p17.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\geq\sqrt{n+1}-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\geq\sqrt{n+1}-2</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.p17.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is the complete bipartite
graph <math id="S1.p17.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{1,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{1,n}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>. Furthermore, for any graph <math id="S1.p17.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>, taking into
account that <math id="S1.p17.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\circ K_{p}\in\mathbf{W}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\circ K_{p}\in\mathbf{W}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, Corollary 2.3]</cite>, the
log-concavity of independent polynomials of <math id="S1.p17.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> graphs can be
further employed in this specific ones. Consequently, it yields that <math id="S1.p17.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(G\circ K_{p};x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(G\circ K_{p};x)</annotation></semantics></math> is log-concave whenever <math id="S1.p17.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> is large enough in correspondence to
<math id="S1.p17.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n(G)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n(G).</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p18.1" class="ltx_p">The paper is organized as follows. In Section <a href="#S2" title="2 Quasi-regularizability of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs ‣ Log-concavity of the independence polynomials of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, we study some
structural properties of <math id="S1.p18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> graphs. Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Log-concavity of independence polynomials of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs ‣ Log-concavity of the independence polynomials of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> presents
findings related to log-concave properties of <math id="S1.p18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(G;x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(G;x)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S1.p18.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>
graphs <math id="S1.p18.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. As an application, the remainder of Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Log-concavity of independence polynomials of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs ‣ Log-concavity of the independence polynomials of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> is
dedicated to examining the log-concavity of <math id="S1.p18.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(H\circ K_{p};x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(H\circ K_{p};x)</annotation></semantics></math> for
sufficiently large <math id="S1.p18.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>. Finally, in Conclusion, we suggest potential
directions for future research.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Quasi-regularizability of <math id="S2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> graphs</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The following characterization of the localization of a
<math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> graph serves as a valuable tool in establishing the proof of
our main theorem in this section. Recall that <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the
family of all well-covered graphs. Several important results are known, as
summarized below:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.1</span></span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a well-covered graph. Then</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S2.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">[</span><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">]</span></cite><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> </span><math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{v}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is well-covered and </span><math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha(G_{v})=\alpha(G)-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha(G_{v})=\alpha(G)-1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">
for all </span><math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V(G)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">[</span><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, Lemma 1]</span></cite><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> If </span><math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is an independent set of </span><math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, then </span><math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{S}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">
is well-covered and </span><math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha(G)=\alpha(G_{S})+|S|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha(G)=\alpha(G_{S})+|S|</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.2</span></span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> graph with <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha(G)&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha(G)&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>. Then</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S2.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">[</span><a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a><span id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, Theorem 2]</span></cite><span id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> </span><math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{v}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is also a </span><math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> graph for
all </span><math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V(G)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">[</span><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a><span id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, Lemma 3.3]</span></cite><span id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> If </span><math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is an independent set of </span><math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|S\right|&lt;\alpha(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|S\right|&lt;\alpha(G)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, then </span><math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{S}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is also a </span><math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> graph.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Furthermore, Staples offered essential evaluations for the general case of the
<math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> class.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.3</span></span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">[</span><a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">]</span></cite><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">  Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>
graph if and only if <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G-v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G-v</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{p-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{p-1}</annotation></semantics></math> graph and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha(G)=\alpha(G-v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha(G)=\alpha(G-v)</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V(G)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.4</span></span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">[</span><a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">]</span></cite><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">  Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a connected
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> graph of order <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> with the independence number <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then the following assertions are true.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S2.I3" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I3.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(i)</span> 
<div id="S2.I3.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I3.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I3.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq p\cdot\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq p\cdot\alpha</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I3.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">. In particular, </span><math id="S2.I3.ix1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=p\cdot\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=p\cdot\alpha</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I3.ix1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> if and only
if </span><math id="S2.I3.ix1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I3.ix1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a complete graph on </span><math id="S2.I3.ix1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I3.ix1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> vertices.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I3.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(ii)</span> 
<div id="S2.I3.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I3.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I3.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If </span><math id="S2.I3.ix2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I3.ix2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, then </span><math id="S2.I3.ix2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta(G)\geq p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta(G)\geq p</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I3.ix2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Clearly, Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem4" title="Lemma 2.4 ‣ 2 Quasi-regularizability of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs ‣ Log-concavity of the independence polynomials of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a> holds true for well-covered graphs as well.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.5</span></span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> graph, then every connected
component of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> contains at least <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> vertices, and, consequently, <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n(G)\geq p\cdot c\left(G\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n(G)\geq p\cdot c\left(G\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\left(G\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\left(G\right)</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of
connected components of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof.</span> By the definition of <math id="S2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> graphs, <math id="S2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n(G)\geq p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n(G)\geq p</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{1},v_{2},\ldots,v_{p}\in V\left(G\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{1},v_{2},\ldots,v_{p}\in V\left(G\right)</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, there exists <math id="S2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> pairwise
disjoint maximum independent sets of <math id="S2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, say <math id="S2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{1},S_{2},\ldots,S_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{1},S_{2},\ldots,S_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>, such
that <math id="S2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}\in S_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}\in S_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq p</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> be a connected component of <math id="S2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. Due to the maximum of the independent
set <math id="S2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|S_{i}\cap V(H)\right|&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|S_{i}\cap V(H)\right|&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq p</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, <math id="S2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{1}\cap V(H),S_{2}\cap V(H),\ldots,S_{p}\cap V(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{1}\cap V(H),S_{2}\cap V(H),\ldots,S_{p}\cap V(H)</annotation></semantics></math> are <math id="S2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> pairwise disjoint non-empty maximum independent sets in
<math id="S2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>. This implies that <math id="S2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n(H)\geq p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n(H)\geq p</annotation></semantics></math>, and consequently, <math id="S2.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n(G)\geq p\cdot c(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n(G)\geq p\cdot c(G)</annotation></semantics></math>.
 <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.6</span></span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A graph is <math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if each of its
every connected component is also <math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof.</span> Let us start by proving that if every connected component of a graph <math id="S2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is
<math id="S2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> itself is <math id="S2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>. Indeed, for each
connected component <math id="S2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S2.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n(G)\geq n(H)\geq p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n(G)\geq n(H)\geq p</annotation></semantics></math>. Now, let
<math id="S2.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{1},\ldots,A_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{1},\ldots,A_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> be <math id="S2.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> pairwise disjoint independent sets in <math id="S2.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. Then,
<math id="S2.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{1}\cap V\left(H\right),\ldots,A_{p}\cap V\left(H\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{1}\cap V\left(H\right),\ldots,A_{p}\cap V\left(H\right)</annotation></semantics></math> may be
enlarged to <math id="S2.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> pairwise disjoint maximum independent sets <math id="S2.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{1}^{H},\ldots,S_{p}^{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>H</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>H</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{1}^{H},\ldots,S_{p}^{H}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>, because <math id="S2.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S2.p6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\in" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∈</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\in</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S2.p6.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>. Consequently,</p>
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="A_{1}\subseteq{\displaystyle\bigcup\limits_{H}}S_{1}^{H},A_{2}\subseteq{\displaystyle\bigcup\limits_{H}}S_{2}^{H},\ldots,A_{p}\subseteq{\displaystyle\bigcup\limits_{H}}S_{p}^{H}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">⊆</mo><mo movablelimits="false">⋃</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>H</mi></msub><mi>S</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>H</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">⊆</mo><mo movablelimits="false">⋃</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>H</mi></msub><mi>S</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>H</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">⊆</mo><mo movablelimits="false">⋃</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>H</mi></msub><mi>S</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>H</mi></msup><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{1}\subseteq{\displaystyle\bigcup\limits_{H}}S_{1}^{H},A_{2}\subseteq{\displaystyle\bigcup\limits_{H}}S_{2}^{H},\ldots,A_{p}\subseteq{\displaystyle\bigcup\limits_{H}}S_{p}^{H},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p6.2" class="ltx_p">where</p>
<table id="S2.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\displaystyle\bigcup\limits_{H}}S_{1}^{H},{\displaystyle\bigcup\limits_{H}}S_{2}^{H},\ldots,{\displaystyle\bigcup\limits_{H}}S_{p}^{H}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⋃</mo><mi>H</mi></munder><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>H</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⋃</mo><mi>H</mi></munder><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>H</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⋃</mo><mi>H</mi></munder><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>H</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\displaystyle\bigcup\limits_{H}}S_{1}^{H},{\displaystyle\bigcup\limits_{H}}S_{2}^{H},\ldots,{\displaystyle\bigcup\limits_{H}}S_{p}^{H}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p6.3" class="ltx_p">are <math id="S2.p6.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> pairwise disjoint maximum independent sets in <math id="S2.p6.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Conversely, let <math id="S2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> be an arbitrary connected component of <math id="S2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. Appying Lemma
<a href="#S2.Thmtheorem5" title="Lemma 2.5 ‣ 2 Quasi-regularizability of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs ‣ Log-concavity of the independence polynomials of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a>, we have <math id="S2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n(H)\geq p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n(H)\geq p</annotation></semantics></math>. Second, let <math id="S2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{1},\ldots,A_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{1},\ldots,A_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>
be <math id="S2.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> pairwise disjoint independent sets in <math id="S2.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>. Since each <math id="S2.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is also
an independent set in <math id="S2.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, there exist <math id="S2.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> pairwise disjoint maximum
independent sets <math id="S2.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{1},\ldots,S_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{1},\ldots,S_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}\subseteq S_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}\subseteq S_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq p</annotation></semantics></math>, given that <math id="S2.p7.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\in\mathbf{W}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\in\mathbf{W}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>. Consequently,
<math id="S2.p7.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{1}\cap V(H),\ldots,S_{p}\cap V(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{1}\cap V(H),\ldots,S_{p}\cap V(H)</annotation></semantics></math> are <math id="S2.p7.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> pairwise disjoint maximum
independent sets in <math id="S2.p7.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.p7.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}\subseteq S_{i}\cap V(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}\subseteq S_{i}\cap V(H)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.p7.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq p</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, <math id="S2.p7.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> belongs to <math id="S2.p7.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> as well.
 <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">We are now in a position to prove the main theorem of this section. First, let
us present a further essential localization property of <math id="S2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> class.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.7</span></span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> graph. The following assertions are true:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S2.I4" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I4.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(i)</span> 
<div id="S2.I4.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I4.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I4.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">if </span><math id="S2.I4.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha(G)&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha(G)&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I4.ix1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, then </span><math id="S2.I4.ix1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{x}\in\mathbf{W}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{x}\in\mathbf{W}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I4.ix1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for every </span><math id="S2.I4.ix1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in V(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in V(G)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I4.ix1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">;</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I4.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(ii)</span> 
<div id="S2.I4.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I4.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I4.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">if all connected components of </span><math id="S2.I4.ix2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I4.ix2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> have an independence number
greater than </span><math id="S2.I4.ix2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I4.ix2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, then </span><math id="S2.I4.ix2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta(G)\geq p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta(G)\geq p</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I4.ix2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">;</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I4.ix3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(iii)</span> 
<div id="S2.I4.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I4.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I4.ix3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">if </span><math id="S2.I4.ix3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I4.ix3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is an independent set of </span><math id="S2.I4.ix3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I4.ix3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> such that </span><math id="S2.I4.ix3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|S\right|&lt;\alpha(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|S\right|&lt;\alpha(G)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I4.ix3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, then </span><math id="S2.I4.ix3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{S}\in\mathbf{W}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{S}\in\mathbf{W}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I4.ix3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">. In particular, if
</span><math id="S2.I4.ix3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I4.ix3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, then </span><math id="S2.I4.ix3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{S}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I4.ix3.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> has no isolated vertices.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof.</span> <em id="S2.p9.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">(i)</em> By Lemmas <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma 2.1 ‣ 2 Quasi-regularizability of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs ‣ Log-concavity of the independence polynomials of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a> and <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem2" title="Lemma 2.2 ‣ 2 Quasi-regularizability of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs ‣ Log-concavity of the independence polynomials of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>, the assertion holds for
<math id="S2.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=1,2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=1,2</annotation></semantics></math>. Now we prove by induction on <math id="S2.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>. Assume that <math id="S2.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&gt;2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&gt;2</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S2.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in V(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in V(G)</annotation></semantics></math>.
According to Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem3" title="Lemma 2.3 ‣ 2 Quasi-regularizability of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs ‣ Log-concavity of the independence polynomials of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>, it is enough to show that <math id="S2.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{x}-v\in\mathbf{W}_{p-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{x}-v\in\mathbf{W}_{p-1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha(G_{x}-v)=\alpha(G_{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha(G_{x}-v)=\alpha(G_{x})</annotation></semantics></math>, for all <math id="S2.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V(G_{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V(G_{x})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p10.1" class="ltx_p">First, we observe that <math id="S2.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{x}-v=(G-v)_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{x}-v=(G-v)_{x}</annotation></semantics></math>, as shown below:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{x}-v=G-N_{G}\left[x\right]-v=G-v-N_{G-v}\left[x\right]=(G-v)_{x}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{x}-v=G-N_{G}\left[x\right]-v=G-v-N_{G-v}\left[x\right]=(G-v)_{x}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p11.1" class="ltx_p">By Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem3" title="Lemma 2.3 ‣ 2 Quasi-regularizability of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs ‣ Log-concavity of the independence polynomials of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>, <math id="S2.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G-v\in\mathbf{W}_{p-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G-v\in\mathbf{W}_{p-1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha(G)=\alpha(G-v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha(G)=\alpha(G-v)</annotation></semantics></math>. By the induction hypothesis, <math id="S2.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(G-v)_{x}\in\mathbf{W}_{p-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(G-v)_{x}\in\mathbf{W}_{p-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that
<math id="S2.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is well-covered and thus <math id="S2.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha(G_{x})=\alpha(G)-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha(G_{x})=\alpha(G)-1</annotation></semantics></math> in accordance with
Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma 2.1 ‣ 2 Quasi-regularizability of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs ‣ Log-concavity of the independence polynomials of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p12.1" class="ltx_p">Moreover, since <math id="S2.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&gt;2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&gt;2</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S2.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G-v\in\mathbf{W}_{p-1}\subseteq\mathbf{W}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G-v\in\mathbf{W}_{p-1}\subseteq\mathbf{W}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>
which means that <math id="S2.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G-v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G-v</annotation></semantics></math> is well-covered. Thus, <math id="S2.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha((G-v)_{x})=\alpha(G-v)-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha((G-v)_{x})=\alpha(G-v)-1</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence,</p>
<table id="S2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\alpha(G_{x}-v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\alpha(G_{x}-v)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\alpha((G-v)_{x})=\alpha(G-v)-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\alpha((G-v)_{x})=\alpha(G-v)-1</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\alpha(G)-1=\alpha(G_{x})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\alpha(G)-1=\alpha(G_{x}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S2.p13.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">(ii)</em> By Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem6" title="Theorem 2.6 ‣ 2 Quasi-regularizability of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs ‣ Log-concavity of the independence polynomials of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.6</span></a>, all connected components of <math id="S2.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>
belong to <math id="S2.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>. Consequently, the assertion follows directly from
Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem4" title="Lemma 2.4 ‣ 2 Quasi-regularizability of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs ‣ Log-concavity of the independence polynomials of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a><em id="S2.p13.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">(ii)</em>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S2.p14.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">(iii)</em> We prove by induction on <math id="S2.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|S\right|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|S\right|</annotation></semantics></math>. If
<math id="S2.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>, then the assertion holds trivially. Now suppose <math id="S2.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\neq\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\neq\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>. Choose <math id="S2.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in S</annotation></semantics></math> and define <math id="S2.p14.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{\prime}=S-\{x\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{\prime}=S-\{x\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, we have</p>
<table id="S2.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{S}=G_{x}-N_{G_{x}}[S^{\prime}]=(G_{x})_{S^{\prime}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{S}=G_{x}-N_{G_{x}}[S^{\prime}]=(G_{x})_{S^{\prime}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p14.2" class="ltx_p">By the assertion <em id="S2.p14.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">(i)</em>, <math id="S2.p14.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{x}\in\mathbf{W}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{x}\in\mathbf{W}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>. Applying the induction
hypothesis, <math id="S2.p14.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(G_{x})_{S^{\prime}}\in\mathbf{W}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(G_{x})_{S^{\prime}}\in\mathbf{W}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> and, hence, <math id="S2.p14.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{S}\in\mathbf{W}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{S}\in\mathbf{W}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p15.1" class="ltx_p">If a connected component of <math id="S2.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> is a complete graph, then its order is <math id="S2.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> at least. Otherwise, it has no isolated vertices in accordance with the
assertion <em id="S2.p15.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">(ii)</em>.
 <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.8</span></span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a connected <math id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> graph with <math id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\neq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\neq 2</annotation></semantics></math>. Then
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>-quasi-regularizable if and only if <math id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n(G)\geq(p+1)\cdot\alpha(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n(G)\geq(p+1)\cdot\alpha(G)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p16" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p16.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p16.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof.</span> Suppose <math id="S2.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>-quasi-regularizable. Hence, if <math id="S2.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is a maximum independent
set of <math id="S2.p16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, then we infer that</p>
<table id="S2.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n(G)=\left|N_{G}(S)\right|+\left|S\right|\geq p\cdot\left|S\right|+\left|S\right|=\left(p+1\right)\cdot\left|S\right|=\left(p+1\right)\cdot\alpha(G)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n(G)=\left|N_{G}(S)\right|+\left|S\right|\geq p\cdot\left|S\right|+\left|S\right|=\left(p+1\right)\cdot\left|S\right|=\left(p+1\right)\cdot\alpha(G).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p17.1" class="ltx_p">Conversely, suppose that <math id="S2.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n(G)\geq(p+1)\cdot\alpha(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n(G)\geq(p+1)\cdot\alpha(G)</annotation></semantics></math>. Actually, the case
<math id="S2.p17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=1</annotation></semantics></math> is Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 1.3 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Log-concavity of the independence polynomials of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a>. Assume that <math id="S2.p17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>. Clearly, if <math id="S2.p17.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha(G)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha(G)=1</annotation></semantics></math>,
then <math id="S2.p17.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is a complete graph of order <math id="S2.p17.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n(G)\geq p+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n(G)\geq p+1</annotation></semantics></math>, and so <math id="S2.p17.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|N_{G}(x)\right|\geq p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|N_{G}(x)\right|\geq p</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S2.p17.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in V(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in V(G)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p18.1" class="ltx_p">Suppose that <math id="S2.p18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha(G)&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha(G)&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S2.p18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> be a non-empty independent set of <math id="S2.p18.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. If
<math id="S2.p18.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|S\right|=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|S\right|=1</annotation></semantics></math>, by Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem4" title="Lemma 2.4 ‣ 2 Quasi-regularizability of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs ‣ Log-concavity of the independence polynomials of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a><em id="S2.p18.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">(ii)</em>, <math id="S2.p18.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|N_{G}(S)\right|\geq p\cdot|S|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|N_{G}(S)\right|\geq p\cdot|S|</annotation></semantics></math>, as required.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p19.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S2.p19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|S\right|=\alpha(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|S\right|=\alpha(G)</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S2.p19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(G)=S\cup N_{G}(S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(G)=S\cup N_{G}(S)</annotation></semantics></math> and so
<math id="S2.p19.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|N_{G}(S)\right|=n(G)-\left|S\right|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|N_{G}(S)\right|=n(G)-\left|S\right|</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence,
<math id="S2.p19.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\cdot\left|S\right|\leq|N_{G}(S)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\cdot\left|S\right|\leq|N_{G}(S)|</annotation></semantics></math>, whenever <math id="S2.p19.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n(G)\geq(p+1)\cdot\alpha(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n(G)\geq(p+1)\cdot\alpha(G)</annotation></semantics></math>, as required.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p20" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p20.1" class="ltx_p">Now we concentrate on the situation when <math id="S2.p20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1&lt;\left|S\right|&lt;\alpha(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1&lt;\left|S\right|&lt;\alpha(G)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S2.p20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha(G)\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha(G)\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S2.p20.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in S</annotation></semantics></math> be chosen arbitrarily. By
Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem7" title="Lemma 2.7 ‣ 2 Quasi-regularizability of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs ‣ Log-concavity of the independence polynomials of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.7</span></a><em id="S2.p20.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">(i)</em>, it follows that <math id="S2.p20.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{x}\in\mathbf{W}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{x}\in\mathbf{W}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S2.p20.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha(G_{x})=\alpha(G)-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha(G_{x})=\alpha(G)-1</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, <math id="S2.p20.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S-x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S-x</annotation></semantics></math> is a non-empty independent set of
<math id="S2.p20.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{x}</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, by Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem7" title="Lemma 2.7 ‣ 2 Quasi-regularizability of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs ‣ Log-concavity of the independence polynomials of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.7</span></a><em id="S2.p20.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">(iii)</em>, <math id="S2.p20.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{S-x}\in\mathbf{W}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{S-x}\in\mathbf{W}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, by Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma 2.1 ‣ 2 Quasi-regularizability of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs ‣ Log-concavity of the independence polynomials of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a><em id="S2.p20.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">(ii)</em>, <math id="S2.p20.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha(G_{S-x})=\alpha(G)-\left|S-x\right|=\alpha(G)-\left|S\right|+1&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha(G_{S-x})=\alpha(G)-\left|S-x\right|=\alpha(G)-\left|S\right|+1&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>. Let</p>
<table id="S2.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{x}=N_{G}(x)-N_{G}(S-x)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{x}=N_{G}(x)-N_{G}(S-x).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.p21" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p21.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p21.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Claim 1.</span> <math id="S2.p21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|X_{x}\right|&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|X_{x}\right|&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p22" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p22.1" class="ltx_p">Assume, to the contrary, that <math id="S2.p22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{x}=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{x}=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>. This implies that
<math id="S2.p22.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{G}(x)\subseteq N_{G}(S-x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{G}(x)\subseteq N_{G}(S-x)</annotation></semantics></math>, and, therefore, <math id="S2.p22.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{G}(S)=N_{G}(S-x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{G}(S)=N_{G}(S-x)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Consequently,</p>
<table id="S2.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(G_{S-x})=\left(V(G)-N_{G}[S]\right)\cup\left\{x\right\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(G_{S-x})=\left(V(G)-N_{G}[S]\right)\cup\left\{x\right\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p22.2" class="ltx_p">which indicates that <math id="S2.p22.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> is an isolated vertex of <math id="S2.p22.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{S-x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{S-x}</annotation></semantics></math>. This is a
contradiction due to Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem7" title="Lemma 2.7 ‣ 2 Quasi-regularizability of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs ‣ Log-concavity of the independence polynomials of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.7</span></a><em id="S2.p22.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">(ii)</em>. Therefore, the set of
private neighbours of <math id="S2.p22.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> is not empty, i.e., <math id="S2.p22.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|X_{x}\right|&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|X_{x}\right|&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p23" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p23.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p23.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Claim 2.</span> If <math id="S2.p23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|X_{x}\right|\leq p-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|X_{x}\right|\leq p-1</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S2.p23.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|X_{x}\right|=p-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|X_{x}\right|=p-1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p24" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p24.1" class="ltx_p">In this case, <math id="S2.p24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> is a vertex of degree at most <math id="S2.p24.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p-1</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.p24.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{S-x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{S-x}</annotation></semantics></math>. However,
<math id="S2.p24.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{S-x}\in\mathbf{W}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{S-x}\in\mathbf{W}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>, and so, by Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem7" title="Lemma 2.7 ‣ 2 Quasi-regularizability of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs ‣ Log-concavity of the independence polynomials of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.7</span></a><em id="S2.p24.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">(ii)</em>, the
induced subgraph on <math id="S2.p24.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{x\}\cup X_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x\}\cup X_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.p24.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{S-x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{S-x}</annotation></semantics></math> is a complete graph <math id="S2.p24.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence <math id="S2.p24.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg_{G_{S-x}}(x)=\left|X_{x}\right|=p-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>deg</mi><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg_{G_{S-x}}(x)=\left|X_{x}\right|=p-1</annotation></semantics></math>, as required.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p25" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p25.1" class="ltx_p">Now, we consider the case where there exists a vertex <math id="S2.p25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in S</annotation></semantics></math>
such that <math id="S2.p25.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|X_{x}\right|\geq p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|X_{x}\right|\geq p</annotation></semantics></math>. We assert that the inequality
<math id="S2.p25.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\cdot\left|S-x\right|\leq\left|N_{G}(S-x)\right|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\cdot\left|S-x\right|\leq\left|N_{G}(S-x)\right|</annotation></semantics></math>
implies the inequality <math id="S2.p25.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\cdot\left|S\right|\leq\left|N_{G}(S)\right|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\cdot\left|S\right|\leq\left|N_{G}(S)\right|</annotation></semantics></math>, because</p>
<table id="S2.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p\cdot\left|S-x\right|\leq\left|N_{G}(S-x)\right|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p\cdot\left|S-x\right|\leq\left|N_{G}(S-x)\right|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Leftrightarrow p\cdot(\left|S\right|-1)\leq\left|N_{G}(S)\right|-\left|X_{x}\right|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">⇔</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Leftrightarrow p\cdot(\left|S\right|-1)\leq\left|N_{G}(S)\right|-\left|X_{x}\right|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Leftrightarrow p\cdot\left|S\right|+(\left|X_{x}\right|-p)\leq\left|N_{G}(S)\right|." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">⇔</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Leftrightarrow p\cdot\left|S\right|+(\left|X_{x}\right|-p)\leq\left|N_{G}(S)\right|.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.p26" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p26.1" class="ltx_p">In other words, for every independent set <math id="S2.p26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.p26.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.p26.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|S\right|&lt;\alpha(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|S\right|&lt;\alpha(G)</annotation></semantics></math>, and any vertex <math id="S2.p26.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in S</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.p26.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|X_{x}\right|\geq p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|X_{x}\right|\geq p</annotation></semantics></math>, the inequality <math id="S2.p26.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\cdot\left|S-x\right|\leq\left|N_{G}(S-x)\right|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\cdot\left|S-x\right|\leq\left|N_{G}(S-x)\right|</annotation></semantics></math> implies <math id="S2.p26.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\cdot\left|S\right|\leq\left|N_{G}(S)\right|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\cdot\left|S\right|\leq\left|N_{G}(S)\right|</annotation></semantics></math> as well.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p27" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p27.1" class="ltx_p">Now, apply this procedure to all vertices <math id="S2.p27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> in the set <math id="S2.p27.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> for which
<math id="S2.p27.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|X_{x}\right|\geq p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|X_{x}\right|\geq p</annotation></semantics></math>. If every vertex in <math id="S2.p27.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies
<math id="S2.p27.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|X_{x}\right|\geq p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|X_{x}\right|\geq p</annotation></semantics></math>, the problem reduces to proving that
<math id="S2.p27.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=p\cdot\left|A\right|\leq|N_{G}(A)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=p\cdot\left|A\right|\leq|N_{G}(A)|</annotation></semantics></math> for all single-vertex subsets
<math id="S2.p27.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.p27.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(G)</annotation></semantics></math>. This holds by Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem4" title="Lemma 2.4 ‣ 2 Quasi-regularizability of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs ‣ Log-concavity of the independence polynomials of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a><em id="S2.p27.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">(ii)</em>, since <math id="S2.p27.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is
connected. Thus, by <span id="S2.p27.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Claim 2</span>, we may assume that <math id="S2.p27.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|X_{x}\right|=p-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|X_{x}\right|=p-1</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S2.p27.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in S</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p28" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p28.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p28.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fact 1.</span> <math id="S2.p28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="ab\notin E(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ab\notin E(G)</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S2.p28.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in{\textstyle\bigcup\limits_{x\in S}}X_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">∈</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⋃</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in{\textstyle\bigcup\limits_{x\in S}}X_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p28.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\in G_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\in G_{S}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p29" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p29.1" class="ltx_p">Indeed, the graph <math id="S2.p29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{S-x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{S-x}</annotation></semantics></math> has a connected component <math id="S2.p29.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> that contains
<math id="S2.p29.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{x\}\cup X_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x\}\cup X_{x}</annotation></semantics></math>. By Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem7" title="Lemma 2.7 ‣ 2 Quasi-regularizability of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs ‣ Log-concavity of the independence polynomials of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.7</span></a><em id="S2.p29.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">(iii)</em>, <math id="S2.p29.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{S-x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{S-x}</annotation></semantics></math> belongs to
<math id="S2.p29.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>, and then Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem6" title="Theorem 2.6 ‣ 2 Quasi-regularizability of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs ‣ Log-concavity of the independence polynomials of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.6</span></a> guarantees that <math id="S2.p29.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is
also in <math id="S2.p29.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>. If there is an edge <math id="S2.p29.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="ab" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ab</annotation></semantics></math> connecting <math id="S2.p29.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> to
<math id="S2.p29.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{S}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S2.p29.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{x,b\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x,b\}</annotation></semantics></math> forms an independent set of <math id="S2.p29.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>. Consequently,
<math id="S2.p29.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha(H)&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha(H)&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, and since <math id="S2.p29.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg_{H}(x)=p-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>deg</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg_{H}(x)=p-1</annotation></semantics></math>, which contradicts Lemma
<a href="#S2.Thmtheorem7" title="Lemma 2.7 ‣ 2 Quasi-regularizability of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs ‣ Log-concavity of the independence polynomials of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.7</span></a><em id="S2.p29.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">(ii)</em>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p30" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p30.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p30.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fact 2.</span> <math id="S2.p30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{x}\cap X_{y}=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{x}\cap X_{y}=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math> for all
distinct elements <math id="S2.p30.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x,y\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x,y\in S</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p31" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p31.1" class="ltx_p">Indeed, this fact follows directly from the definition.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p32" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p32.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.p32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U=N_{G}(S)-{\textstyle\bigcup\limits_{x\in S}}X_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⋃</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U=N_{G}(S)-{\textstyle\bigcup\limits_{x\in S}}X_{x}</annotation></semantics></math>. First, we claim that <math id="S2.p32.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\neq\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\neq\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>. Indeed, <math id="S2.p32.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(G_{S})\neq\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(G_{S})\neq\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>, since <math id="S2.p32.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha(G_{S})=\alpha(G)-\left|S\right|&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha(G_{S})=\alpha(G)-\left|S\right|&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. By <em id="S2.p32.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Fact
1</em>, we must have <math id="S2.p32.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\neq\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\neq\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>, because <math id="S2.p32.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is connected. If <math id="S2.p32.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|U\right|\geq\left|S\right|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|U\right|\geq\left|S\right|</annotation></semantics></math>, by <em id="S2.p32.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Fact 2</em>,</p>
<table id="S2.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|N_{G}(S)\right|=\sum_{x\in S}\left|X_{x}\right|+\left|U\right|=(p-1)\cdot\left|S\right|+\left|U\right|\geq p\cdot\left|S\right|." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|N_{G}(S)\right|=\sum_{x\in S}\left|X_{x}\right|+\left|U\right|=(p-1)\cdot\left|S\right|+\left|U\right|\geq p\cdot\left|S\right|.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p32.2" class="ltx_p">Now, we consider the case where <math id="S2.p32.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|U\right|\leq\left|S\right|-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|U\right|\leq\left|S\right|-1</annotation></semantics></math> (and <math id="S2.p32.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|S\right|\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|S\right|\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>). We will show that this
scenario cannot occur. By definition of the set <math id="S2.p32.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{x}</annotation></semantics></math>, every vertex in <math id="S2.p32.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>
has two neighbors in <math id="S2.p32.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> at least. Then there exist two distinct vertices
<math id="S2.p32.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x,y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x,y</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.p32.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> that are adjacent to a vertex <math id="S2.p32.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{xy}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{xy}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.p32.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>, since
<math id="S2.p32.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\neq\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\neq\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S2.p32.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{xy}=U-N_{G}(S-x-y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{xy}=U-N_{G}(S-x-y)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p33" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p33.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S2.p33.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Fact 3.</em> If <math id="S2.p33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{xy}\neq\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{xy}\neq\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S2.p33.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|U_{xy}\right|\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|U_{xy}\right|\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p34" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p34.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.p34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{xy}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{xy}</annotation></semantics></math> be a vertex in <math id="S2.p34.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{xy}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{xy}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S2.p34.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{xy}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{xy}</annotation></semantics></math> is not adjacent
to any vertex in <math id="S2.p34.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S-x-y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S-x-y</annotation></semantics></math> but is adjacent to at least one of <math id="S2.p34.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S2.p34.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>. By the
definition of <math id="S2.p34.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{x}</annotation></semantics></math>, it follows that <math id="S2.p34.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{xy}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{xy}</annotation></semantics></math> must be adjacent to both <math id="S2.p34.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S2.p34.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p35" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p35.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S2.p35.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Case 1. </em> <math id="S2.p35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{G}(u_{xy})\cap V(G_{S})\neq\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{G}(u_{xy})\cap V(G_{S})\neq\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p36" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p36.1" class="ltx_p">In this case, let <math id="S2.p36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> be a maximum independent set in <math id="S2.p36.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> that contains at
least one vertex from <math id="S2.p36.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{G}(u_{xy})\cap V(G_{S})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{G}(u_{xy})\cap V(G_{S})</annotation></semantics></math>. Such <math id="S2.p36.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> exists, because
<math id="S2.p36.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> belongs to <math id="S2.p36.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>, and, consequently, it is well-covered.
Then, <math id="S2.p36.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\cup(S-x-y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\cup(S-x-y)</annotation></semantics></math> is an independent set in <math id="S2.p36.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, which implies that
<math id="S2.p36.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{A\cup(S-x-y)}\in\mathbf{W}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{A\cup(S-x-y)}\in\mathbf{W}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p37" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p37.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S2.p37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|U_{xy}\right|=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|U_{xy}\right|=1</annotation></semantics></math>, say <math id="S2.p37.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{xy}=\{u_{xy}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{xy}=\{u_{xy}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, then the vertex
set of <math id="S2.p37.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{A\cup(S-x-y)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{A\cup(S-x-y)}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.p37.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{x,y\}\cup X_{x}\cup X_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x,y\}\cup X_{x}\cup X_{y}</annotation></semantics></math>. Noting that
<math id="S2.p37.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg_{G_{A\cup(S-x-y)}}(x)=p-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>deg</mi><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg_{G_{A\cup(S-x-y)}}(x)=p-1</annotation></semantics></math>, it follows that <math id="S2.p37.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{A\cup(S-x-y)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{A\cup(S-x-y)}</annotation></semantics></math> is
disconnected, consisting of two connected components <math id="S2.p37.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G[\{x\}\cup X_{x}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G[\{x\}\cup X_{x}]</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S2.p37.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G[\{y\}\cup X_{y}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G[\{y\}\cup X_{y}]</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, both <math id="S2.p37.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G[\{x\}\cup X_{x}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G[\{x\}\cup X_{x}]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p37.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G[\{y\}\cup X_{y}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G[\{y\}\cup X_{y}]</annotation></semantics></math>
are complete graphs <math id="S2.p37.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> and no edges exist between <math id="S2.p37.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p37.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{y}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Clearly, <math id="S2.p37.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{xy}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{xy}</annotation></semantics></math> is adjacent to all vertices in <math id="S2.p37.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{x}\cup X_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{x}\cup X_{y}</annotation></semantics></math>. Indeed,
suppose, to the contrary, that <math id="S2.p37.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{x}-N_{G}[u_{xy}]\neq\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{x}-N_{G}[u_{xy}]\neq\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>
(respectively, <math id="S2.p37.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{y}-N_{G}[u_{xy}]\neq\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{y}-N_{G}[u_{xy}]\neq\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>). Moreover, every vertex in
<math id="S2.p37.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{x}-N_{G}[u_{xy}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{x}-N_{G}[u_{xy}]</annotation></semantics></math> is neither adjacent to any vertex in <math id="S2.p37.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{y}-N_{G}[u_{xy}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{y}-N_{G}[u_{xy}]</annotation></semantics></math> nor in <math id="S2.p37.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(G_{S})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(G_{S})</annotation></semantics></math>, in accordance with <span id="S2.p37.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fact 1</span>. Since
<math id="S2.p37.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{u_{xy}}\in\mathbf{W}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{u_{xy}}\in\mathbf{W}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>, each of its connected components must be of
order <math id="S2.p37.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>, at least, which contradicts the inequality <math id="S2.p37.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\left(G[X_{x}-N_{G}[u_{xy}]]\right)&lt;p-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\left(G[X_{x}-N_{G}[u_{xy}]]\right)&lt;p-1</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, we must have <math id="S2.p37.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{x}-N_{G}[u_{xy}]=X_{y}-N_{G}[u_{xy}]=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{x}-N_{G}[u_{xy}]=X_{y}-N_{G}[u_{xy}]=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>, meaning that <math id="S2.p37.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{xy}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{xy}</annotation></semantics></math> is adjacent to
all vertices in <math id="S2.p37.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{x}\cup X_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{x}\cup X_{y}</annotation></semantics></math>. Now, it follows that <math id="S2.p37.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{(S-x-y)\cup\{u_{xy}\}}=G_{S}-N_{G}[u_{xy}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{(S-x-y)\cup\{u_{xy}\}}=G_{S}-N_{G}[u_{xy}]</annotation></semantics></math>. Consequently, we obtain the following
inequality</p>
<table id="S2.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha(G_{(S-x-y)\cup\{u_{xy}\}})\leq\alpha(G_{S})\Leftrightarrow\alpha(G)-(\left|S\right|-1)\leq\alpha(G)-\left|S\right|," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⇔</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha(G_{(S-x-y)\cup\{u_{xy}\}})\leq\alpha(G_{S})\Leftrightarrow\alpha(G)-(\left|S\right|-1)\leq\alpha(G)-\left|S\right|,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p37.2" class="ltx_p">which is a contradiction. Therefore, we must have <math id="S2.p37.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|U_{xy}\right|\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|U_{xy}\right|\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p38" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p38.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S2.p38.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Case 2. </em> <math id="S2.p38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{G}(u_{xy})\cap V(G_{S})=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{G}(u_{xy})\cap V(G_{S})=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p39" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p39.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S2.p39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|U_{xy}\right|=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|U_{xy}\right|=1</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., <math id="S2.p39.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{xy}=\{u_{xy}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{xy}=\{u_{xy}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, then, by
<span id="S2.p39.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fact 1</span>, <math id="S2.p39.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H=G\left[\{u_{xy},x,y\}\cup X_{x}\cup X_{y}\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H=G\left[\{u_{xy},x,y\}\cup X_{x}\cup X_{y}\right]</annotation></semantics></math> is a
connected component of <math id="S2.p39.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{S-x-y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{S-x-y}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, <math id="S2.p39.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{x,y\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x,y\}</annotation></semantics></math> is a maximal independent
set of <math id="S2.p39.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S2.p39.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\in\mathbf{W}_{p}\subset\mathbf{W}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\in\mathbf{W}_{p}\subset\mathbf{W}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, it follows that
<math id="S2.p39.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{x,y\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x,y\}</annotation></semantics></math> is a maximum independent set of <math id="S2.p39.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>, and, consequently,
<math id="S2.p39.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha(H)=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha(H)=2</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, there exists a vertex <math id="S2.p39.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in X_{x}\cup X_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in X_{x}\cup X_{y}</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="S2.p39.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{u_{xy},a\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{u_{xy},a\}</annotation></semantics></math> is a maximum independent set of <math id="S2.p39.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>. Without loss of
generality, assume that <math id="S2.p39.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in X_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in X_{x}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the subgraph <math id="S2.p39.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{u_{xy}}=H-N_{H}[u_{xy}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{u_{xy}}=H-N_{H}[u_{xy}]</annotation></semantics></math> is a complete graph containing <math id="S2.p39.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>, and similarly,
<math id="S2.p39.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{a}=H-N_{H}[a]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{a}=H-N_{H}[a]</annotation></semantics></math> is a complete graph containing <math id="S2.p39.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{xy}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{xy}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p40" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p40.1" class="ltx_p">First,</p>
<table id="S2.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\left(H_{a}\right)=\{y,u_{xy}\}\cup\left(X_{y}-N_{G}(a)\right)\cup\left(X_{x}-N_{G}(a)-\left\{a\right\}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\left(H_{a}\right)=\{y,u_{xy}\}\cup\left(X_{y}-N_{G}(a)\right)\cup\left(X_{x}-N_{G}(a)-\left\{a\right\}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p40.2" class="ltx_p">Hence, <math id="S2.p40.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{x}-N_{G}(a)-\left\{a\right\}=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{x}-N_{G}(a)-\left\{a\right\}=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>, because <math id="S2.p40.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> is
complete, and <math id="S2.p40.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> is not adjacent to any private neighbor of <math id="S2.p40.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus
<math id="S2.p40.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{x}=N_{G}\left[a\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{x}=N_{G}\left[a\right]</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore,</p>
<table id="S2.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n(H_{a})=\left|\{y,u_{xy}\}\right|+\left|X_{y}-N_{G}(a)\right|=2+\left|X_{y}-N_{G}(a)\right|." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n(H_{a})=\left|\{y,u_{xy}\}\right|+\left|X_{y}-N_{G}(a)\right|=2+\left|X_{y}-N_{G}(a)\right|.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p40.3" class="ltx_p">In addition, <math id="S2.p40.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{y}\cap N_{G}(u_{xy})\supseteq X_{y}-N_{G}(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>⊇</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{y}\cap N_{G}(u_{xy})\supseteq X_{y}-N_{G}(a)</annotation></semantics></math>, because
<math id="S2.p40.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{a,u_{xy}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{a,u_{xy}\}</annotation></semantics></math> is a dominating set in <math id="S2.p40.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>. Consequently, we have</p>
<table id="S2.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2+\left|X_{y}\cap N_{G}(u_{xy})\right|\geq 2+\left|X_{y}-N_{G}(a)\right|=n(H_{a})\geq p," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2+\left|X_{y}\cap N_{G}(u_{xy})\right|\geq 2+\left|X_{y}-N_{G}(a)\right|=n(H_{a})\geq p,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p40.4" class="ltx_p">since <math id="S2.p40.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{a}\in\mathbf{W}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{a}\in\mathbf{W}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>. Finally, <math id="S2.p40.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|X_{y}\cap N_{G}(u_{xy})\right|\geq p-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|X_{y}\cap N_{G}(u_{xy})\right|\geq p-2</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p41" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p41.1" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="S2.p41.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|X_{y}\right|=p-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|X_{y}\right|=p-1</annotation></semantics></math>, there are two options left only:
either <math id="S2.p41.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|X_{y}\cap N_{G}(u_{xy})\right|=p-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|X_{y}\cap N_{G}(u_{xy})\right|=p-1</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S2.p41.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|X_{y}\cap N_{G}(u_{xy})\right|=p-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|X_{y}\cap N_{G}(u_{xy})\right|=p-2</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p42" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p42.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S2.p42.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|X_{y}\cap N_{G}(u_{xy})\right|=p-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|X_{y}\cap N_{G}(u_{xy})\right|=p-1</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S2.p42.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{y}-N_{G}(u_{xy})=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{y}-N_{G}(u_{xy})=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>, because <math id="S2.p42.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|X_{y}\right|=p-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|X_{y}\right|=p-1</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence,</p>
<table id="S2.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n(H_{u_{xy}})=\left|X_{x}-N_{G}(u_{xy})\right|," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n(H_{u_{xy}})=\left|X_{x}-N_{G}(u_{xy})\right|,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p42.2" class="ltx_p">since</p>
<table id="S2.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\left(H_{u_{xy}}\right)=\left(X_{x}-N_{G}(u_{xy})\right)\cup\left(X_{y}-N_{G}(u_{xy})\right)\text{.}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>.</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\left(H_{u_{xy}}\right)=\left(X_{x}-N_{G}(u_{xy})\right)\cup\left(X_{y}-N_{G}(u_{xy})\right)\text{.}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.p43" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p43.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, <math id="S2.p43.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n(H_{u_{xy}})=\left|X_{x}-N_{G}(u_{xy})\right|\leq\left|X_{x}\right|=p-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n(H_{u_{xy}})=\left|X_{x}-N_{G}(u_{xy})\right|\leq\left|X_{x}\right|=p-1</annotation></semantics></math>, which contradicts the assumption that
<math id="S2.p43.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{u_{xy}}\in\mathbf{W}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{u_{xy}}\in\mathbf{W}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p44" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p44.1" class="ltx_p">Therefore, we must have <math id="S2.p44.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|X_{y}\cap N_{G}(u_{xy})\right|=p-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|X_{y}\cap N_{G}(u_{xy})\right|=p-2</annotation></semantics></math>,
implying that <math id="S2.p44.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{xy}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{xy}</annotation></semantics></math> is adjacent to all vertices in <math id="S2.p44.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{y}-b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{y}-b</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S2.p44.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\in X_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\in X_{y}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., <math id="S2.p44.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{y}-N_{G}(u_{xy})=\left\{b\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{y}-N_{G}(u_{xy})=\left\{b\right\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, <math id="S2.p44.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{G}(u_{xy})\cap X_{x}=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{G}(u_{xy})\cap X_{x}=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>, because <math id="S2.p44.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|X_{x}\right|=p-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|X_{x}\right|=p-1</annotation></semantics></math> and</p>
<table id="S2.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|X_{x}-N_{G}(u_{xy})\right|+\left|\left\{b\right\}\right|=n\left(H_{u_{xy}}\right)\geq p," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|X_{x}-N_{G}(u_{xy})\right|+\left|\left\{b\right\}\right|=n\left(H_{u_{xy}}\right)\geq p,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p44.2" class="ltx_p">in order to give <math id="S2.p44.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> a chance to be a <math id="S2.p44.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> graph. Thus, <math id="S2.p44.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\left(H_{u_{xy}}\right)=X_{x}\cup\left\{b\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\left(H_{u_{xy}}\right)=X_{x}\cup\left\{b\right\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, <math id="S2.p44.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{x}\subseteq N_{G}\left(b\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{x}\subseteq N_{G}\left(b\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, since <math id="S2.p44.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{u_{xy}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{u_{xy}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a complete graph.
Consequently, <math id="S2.p44.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{b}</annotation></semantics></math> is a complete graph with</p>
<table id="S2.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\left(H_{b}\right)=\{u_{xy},x\}\cup\left(X_{y}-N_{G}\left[b\right]\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\left(H_{b}\right)=\{u_{xy},x\}\cup\left(X_{y}-N_{G}\left[b\right]\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p44.3" class="ltx_p">which is possible only if <math id="S2.p44.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{y}-N_{G}\left[b\right]=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{y}-N_{G}\left[b\right]=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>, because
and <math id="S2.p44.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> is not adjacent to any private neighbor of <math id="S2.p44.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>. Finally,
<math id="S2.p44.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{b}=\{u_{xy},x\}=K_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{b}=\{u_{xy},x\}=K_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, which belongs to <math id="S2.p44.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>, implying that
either <math id="S2.p44.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=2</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S2.p44.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=1</annotation></semantics></math>. According to the assumption that <math id="S2.p44.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>, this cannot
happen.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p45" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p45.1" class="ltx_p">By <em id="S2.p45.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Fact 3</em>, what is left is to consider the case
<math id="S2.p45.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|U_{xy}\right|=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|U_{xy}\right|=0</annotation></semantics></math>, which implies that <math id="S2.p45.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\subseteq N_{G}(S-x-y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\subseteq N_{G}(S-x-y)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p46" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p46.1" class="ltx_p">Recall that <math id="S2.p46.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\neq\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\neq\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>, because <math id="S2.p46.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is connected. Further, since
<math id="S2.p46.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\subseteq N_{G}(S-x-y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\subseteq N_{G}(S-x-y)</annotation></semantics></math>, there must be a vertex <math id="S2.p46.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\in S-x-y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\in S-x-y</annotation></semantics></math> that is
adjacent to some <math id="S2.p46.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in U</annotation></semantics></math>. Now, let</p>
<table id="S2.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{xyz}=(N_{G}(x)\cap N_{G}(y)\cap N_{G}(z))-N_{G}(S-x-y-z)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{xyz}=(N_{G}(x)\cap N_{G}(y)\cap N_{G}(z))-N_{G}(S-x-y-z).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.p47" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p47.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S2.p47.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Fact 4.</em> Either <math id="S2.p47.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|U_{xz}\right|\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></msub><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|U_{xz}\right|\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, or <math id="S2.p47.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|U_{yz}\right|\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></msub><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|U_{yz}\right|\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, or <math id="S2.p47.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|U_{xyz}\right|\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></msub><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|U_{xyz}\right|\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p48" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p48.1" class="ltx_p">We have</p>
<table id="S2.Ex22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(G_{S-x-y})=\{x,y\}\cup X_{x}\cup X_{y}\cup V(G_{S})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(G_{S-x-y})=\{x,y\}\cup X_{x}\cup X_{y}\cup V(G_{S}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p48.2" class="ltx_p">By Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem7" title="Lemma 2.7 ‣ 2 Quasi-regularizability of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs ‣ Log-concavity of the independence polynomials of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.7</span></a><em id="S2.p48.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">(iii)</em>, <math id="S2.p48.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{S-x-y}\in\mathbf{W}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{S-x-y}\in\mathbf{W}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, the
graph <math id="S2.p48.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{S-x-y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{S-x-y}</annotation></semantics></math> consists of the disjoint union of complete subgraphs on the
vertex sets <math id="S2.p48.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{x\}\cup X_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x\}\cup X_{x}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.p48.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{y\}\cup X_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{y\}\cup X_{y}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the subgraph <math id="S2.p48.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{S}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This implies that both <math id="S2.p48.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p48.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{y}</annotation></semantics></math> are cliques, with no edges
connecting them.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p49" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p49.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S2.p49.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in(N_{G}(z)\cap U)-N_{G}(S-x-y-z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in(N_{G}(z)\cap U)-N_{G}(S-x-y-z)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p49.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\notin U_{xz}\cup U_{yz}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∉</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\notin U_{xz}\cup U_{yz}</annotation></semantics></math>, by the definitions of <math id="S2.p49.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{xz}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{xz}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p49.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{yz}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{yz}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.p49.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> must belong to
both <math id="S2.p49.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{G}(S-x-z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{G}(S-x-z)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p49.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{G}(S-y-z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{G}(S-y-z)</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover <math id="S2.p49.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> is adjacent to
both <math id="S2.p49.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p49.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>, which implies that <math id="S2.p49.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in N_{G}(x)\cap N_{G}(y)\cap N_{G}(z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in N_{G}(x)\cap N_{G}(y)\cap N_{G}(z)</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, we conclude that <math id="S2.p49.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in U_{xyz}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in U_{xyz}</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore, by the
definitions of <math id="S2.p49.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{x},X_{y}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{x},X_{y},</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p49.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{z}</annotation></semantics></math>, it follows that</p>
<table id="S2.Ex23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{xz}\cup U_{xyz}\cup U_{yz}=(N_{G}(z)\cap U)-N_{G}(S-x-y-z)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{xz}\cup U_{xyz}\cup U_{yz}=(N_{G}(z)\cap U)-N_{G}(S-x-y-z),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p49.2" class="ltx_p">and both <math id="S2.p49.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p49.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> are adjacent to all vertices in <math id="S2.p49.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{xz}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{xz}</annotation></semantics></math>, and both <math id="S2.p49.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S2.p49.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> are adjacent to all vertices in <math id="S2.p49.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{yz}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{yz}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, we infer that</p>
<table id="S2.Ex24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(G_{S-x-y-z})=\{x,y,z\}\cup X_{x}\cup X_{y}\cup X_{z}\cup U_{xz}\cup U_{xyz}\cup U_{yz}\cup V(G_{S})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(G_{S-x-y-z})=\{x,y,z\}\cup X_{x}\cup X_{y}\cup X_{z}\cup U_{xz}\cup U_{xyz}\cup U_{yz}\cup V(G_{S}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p49.3" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S2.p49.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{yz}\neq\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{yz}\neq\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. <math id="S2.p49.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{xz}\neq\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{xz}\neq\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>), by <em id="S2.p49.3.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Fact 3</em>, we
know that <math id="S2.p49.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|U_{yz}\right|\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></msub><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|U_{yz}\right|\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. <math id="S2.p49.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|U_{xz}\right|\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></msub><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|U_{xz}\right|\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>), as expected. Conversely, suppose <math id="S2.p49.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{xz}=U_{yz}=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{xz}=U_{yz}=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, <math id="S2.p49.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{xyz}\neq\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{xyz}\neq\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S2.p49.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H=G_{S-x-y-z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H=G_{S-x-y-z}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then,
<math id="S2.p49.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\in\mathbf{W}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\in\mathbf{W}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> and its vertex set is</p>
<table id="S2.Ex25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(H)=\{x,y,z\}\cup X_{x}\cup X_{y}\cup X_{z}\cup U_{xyz}\cup V(G_{S})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(H)=\{x,y,z\}\cup X_{x}\cup X_{y}\cup X_{z}\cup U_{xyz}\cup V(G_{S}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p49.4" class="ltx_p">Since each of <math id="S2.p49.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{x}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.p49.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{y}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.p49.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{z}</annotation></semantics></math> consists of the disjoint union of
two complete graphs with vertex sets <math id="S2.p49.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{x\}\cup X_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x\}\cup X_{x}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.p49.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{y\}\cup X_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{y\}\cup X_{y}</annotation></semantics></math>, and
<math id="S2.p49.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{z\}\cup X_{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>z</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{z\}\cup X_{z}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively, along with the graph <math id="S2.p49.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{S}</annotation></semantics></math>, it follows that
<math id="S2.p49.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{x},X_{y},X_{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>z</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{x},X_{y},X_{z}</annotation></semantics></math> are cliques, and no edges exist between these sets.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p50" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p50.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S2.p50.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|U_{xyz}\right|=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></msub><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|U_{xyz}\right|=1</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S2.p50.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{xyz}=\{u\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{xyz}=\{u\}</annotation></semantics></math>. By Lemma
<a href="#S2.Thmtheorem7" title="Lemma 2.7 ‣ 2 Quasi-regularizability of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs ‣ Log-concavity of the independence polynomials of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.7</span></a><em id="S2.p50.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">(ii)</em>, <math id="S2.p50.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> is adjacent to every vertex in <math id="S2.p50.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{x}\cup X_{y}\cup X_{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>z</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{x}\cup X_{y}\cup X_{z}</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S2.p50.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> is not adjacent to any vertex in <math id="S2.p50.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{S}</annotation></semantics></math>, then the connected
component of <math id="S2.p50.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> containing <math id="S2.p50.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{x,y,z,u\}\cup X_{x}\cup X_{y}\cup X_{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>z</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x,y,z,u\}\cup X_{x}\cup X_{y}\cup X_{z}</annotation></semantics></math> does
not belong to <math id="S2.p50.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>. Conversely, assume <math id="S2.p50.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> is adjacent to a vertex
in <math id="S2.p50.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(G_{S})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(G_{S})</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S2.p50.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{u}=G_{S}-N_{G}(u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{u}=G_{S}-N_{G}(u)</annotation></semantics></math>. This implies that</p>
<table id="S2.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex26"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\alpha(H_{u})\leq\alpha(G_{S})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\alpha(H_{u})\leq\alpha(G_{S})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex26.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Leftrightarrow\alpha(H)-1\leq\alpha(G)-|S|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">⇔</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Leftrightarrow\alpha(H)-1\leq\alpha(G)-|S|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex27"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Leftrightarrow\alpha(G)-\left|S-x-y-z\right|-1\leq\alpha(G)-\left|S\right|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">⇔</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Leftrightarrow\alpha(G)-\left|S-x-y-z\right|-1\leq\alpha(G)-\left|S\right|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex28"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Leftrightarrow\alpha(G)-(\left|S\right|-3)-1\leq\alpha(G)-|S|," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">⇔</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Leftrightarrow\alpha(G)-(\left|S\right|-3)-1\leq\alpha(G)-|S|,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p50.2" class="ltx_p">a contradiction.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p51" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p51.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S2.p51.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|U_{xyz}\right|=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></msub><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|U_{xyz}\right|=2</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S2.p51.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{xyz}=\{u,v\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{xyz}=\{u,v\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Recall that
<math id="S2.p51.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H=G_{S-x-y-z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H=G_{S-x-y-z}</annotation></semantics></math> and</p>
<table id="S2.Ex29" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(H)=\{u,v,x,y,z\}\cup X_{x}\cup X_{y}\cup X_{z}\cup V(G_{S})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(H)=\{u,v,x,y,z\}\cup X_{x}\cup X_{y}\cup X_{z}\cup V(G_{S}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p51.2" class="ltx_p">Examining the structure, we observe that <math id="S2.p51.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{x},X_{y}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{x},X_{y},</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p51.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{z}</annotation></semantics></math> are
cliques and there are no edges between these sets. If neither <math id="S2.p51.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> nor <math id="S2.p51.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> is
adjacent to any vertex in <math id="S2.p51.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{S}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S2.p51.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> consists of the disjoint union of
the connected component <math id="S2.p51.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> with vertex set <math id="S2.p51.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{u,v,x,y,z\}\cup X_{x}\cup X_{y}\cup X_{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>z</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{u,v,x,y,z\}\cup X_{x}\cup X_{y}\cup X_{z}</annotation></semantics></math> and the graph <math id="S2.p51.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{S}</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that <math id="S2.p51.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha(K)=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha(K)=3</annotation></semantics></math> and the sizes
of <math id="S2.p51.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X_{x}-N_{K}(u))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X_{x}-N_{K}(u))</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.p51.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X_{y}-N_{K}(u))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X_{y}-N_{K}(u))</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.p51.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X_{z}-N_{K}(u))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X_{z}-N_{K}(u))</annotation></semantics></math> are at most
<math id="S2.p51.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p-1</annotation></semantics></math>. Consequently, <math id="S2.p51.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> does not belong to <math id="S2.p51.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>. This implies
that at least one of <math id="S2.p51.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S2.p51.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> must be adjacent to a vertex in <math id="S2.p51.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{S}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Suppose that <math id="S2.p51.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> is adjacent to a vertex in <math id="S2.p51.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{S}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S2.p51.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> be a maximum
independent set of <math id="S2.p51.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> that contains exactly one vertex from <math id="S2.p51.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{G}(u)\cap V(G_{S})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{G}(u)\cap V(G_{S})</annotation></semantics></math>. We consider the following two cases.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p52" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p52.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S2.p52.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Case 1.</em> <math id="S2.p52.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> is not adjacent to a vertex in <math id="S2.p52.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p53" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p53.1" class="ltx_p">In this
case, <math id="S2.p53.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{A}</annotation></semantics></math> is a graph in <math id="S2.p53.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> induced by the vertex set
<math id="S2.p53.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{v,x,y,z\}\cup X_{x}\cup X_{y}\cup X_{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>z</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{v,x,y,z\}\cup X_{x}\cup X_{y}\cup X_{z}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.p53.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{x,y,z\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x,y,z\}</annotation></semantics></math> forms a
domination set of <math id="S2.p53.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{A}</annotation></semantics></math>. Consequently, <math id="S2.p53.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{A\cup\{v\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{A\cup\{v\}}</annotation></semantics></math> belongs to
<math id="S2.p53.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> and is induced by the set <math id="S2.p53.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X_{x}-N_{H}(v))\cup(X_{y}-N_{H}(v))\cup(X_{z}-N_{H}(v))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X_{x}-N_{H}(v))\cup(X_{y}-N_{H}(v))\cup(X_{z}-N_{H}(v))</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S2.p53.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha(H_{A\cup\{v\}})=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha(H_{A\cup\{v\}})=2</annotation></semantics></math>, which is a contradiction.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p54" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p54.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S2.p54.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Case 2.</em> <math id="S2.p54.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> is adjacent to any vertex in <math id="S2.p54.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p55" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p55.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S2.p55.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Case 2.1.</em> <math id="S2.p55.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="uv\notin E(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">uv\notin E(G)</annotation></semantics></math>. In this case, we
have</p>
<table id="S2.Ex30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(H_{u})=\{v\}\cup(X_{x}-N_{G}(u))\cup(X_{y}-N_{G}(u))\cup(X_{z}-N_{G}(u))\cup V(G_{S}-N_{G}(u))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(H_{u})=\{v\}\cup(X_{x}-N_{G}(u))\cup(X_{y}-N_{G}(u))\cup(X_{z}-N_{G}(u))\cup V(G_{S}-N_{G}(u)).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p55.2" class="ltx_p">Because <math id="S2.p55.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{x}-N_{G}(u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{x}-N_{G}(u)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.p55.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{y}-N_{G}(u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{y}-N_{G}(u)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p55.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{z}-N_{G}(u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{z}-N_{G}(u)</annotation></semantics></math> are
cliques of size at most <math id="S2.p55.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p-1</annotation></semantics></math> with no edges between them, and because <math id="S2.p55.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{u}</annotation></semantics></math>
is a <math id="S2.p55.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> graph, we may assume without loss of generality that
<math id="S2.p55.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{x}-N_{G}(u)=X_{y}-N_{G}(u)=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{x}-N_{G}(u)=X_{y}-N_{G}(u)=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>, leaving only <math id="S2.p55.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{z}-N_{G}(u)=X_{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>z</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{z}-N_{G}(u)=X_{z}</annotation></semantics></math>. This means that <math id="S2.p55.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> is connected to every vertex in
<math id="S2.p55.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{z}</annotation></semantics></math> within <math id="S2.p55.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{u}</annotation></semantics></math>. Consequently, <math id="S2.p55.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\{u,v\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\{u,v\}}</annotation></semantics></math> is an induced subgraph
of <math id="S2.p55.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> on the vertex set <math id="S2.p55.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{S}-N_{G}(u)-N_{G}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{S}-N_{G}(u)-N_{G}(v)</annotation></semantics></math>, which leads to the
bound <math id="S2.p55.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha(H_{\{u,v\}})\leq\alpha(G)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha(H_{\{u,v\}})\leq\alpha(G).</annotation></semantics></math> Thus, we obtain the inequality
<math id="S2.p55.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha(G)-|S-x-y-z|-2\leq\alpha(G)-|S|," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha(G)-|S-x-y-z|-2\leq\alpha(G)-|S|,</annotation></semantics></math> which leads to a contradiction.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p56" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p56.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S2.p56.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Case 2.2.</em> <math id="S2.p56.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="uv\in E(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">uv\in E(G)</annotation></semantics></math>. In this case, we have</p>
<table id="S2.Ex31" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(H_{u})=(X_{x}-N_{G}(u))\cup(X_{y}-N_{G}(u))\cup(X_{z}-N_{G}(u))\cup V(G_{S}-N_{G}(u))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(H_{u})=(X_{x}-N_{G}(u))\cup(X_{y}-N_{G}(u))\cup(X_{z}-N_{G}(u))\cup V(G_{S}-N_{G}(u)).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p56.2" class="ltx_p">Therefore, we have <math id="S2.p56.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{x}=X_{y}=X_{z}=N_{G}(u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{x}=X_{y}=X_{z}=N_{G}(u)</annotation></semantics></math>, meaning that <math id="S2.p56.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> is
adjacent to every vertex in <math id="S2.p56.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{x}\cup X_{y}\cup X_{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>z</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{x}\cup X_{y}\cup X_{z}</annotation></semantics></math>. Consequently,
<math id="S2.p56.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{u}=G_{S}-N_{G}(u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{u}=G_{S}-N_{G}(u)</annotation></semantics></math> is an induced subgraph of <math id="S2.p56.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{S}</annotation></semantics></math>. This gives the
inequality <math id="S2.p56.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha(H_{u})\leq\alpha(G_{S})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha(H_{u})\leq\alpha(G_{S}),</annotation></semantics></math> which simplifies to <math id="S2.p56.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha(G)-|S-x-y-z|-1\leq\alpha(G)-|S|," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha(G)-|S-x-y-z|-1\leq\alpha(G)-|S|,</annotation></semantics></math> leading to a contradiction.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p57" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p57.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S2.p57.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Fact 5.</em> For any distinct elements <math id="S2.p57.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x,y,x^{\prime},y^{\prime},z^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x,y,x^{\prime},y^{\prime},z^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.p57.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S2.p57.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{xy}\cap U_{x^{\prime}y^{\prime}}=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{xy}\cap U_{x^{\prime}y^{\prime}}=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p57.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{xy}\cap U_{x^{\prime}y^{\prime}z^{\prime}}=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{xy}\cap U_{x^{\prime}y^{\prime}z^{\prime}}=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p58" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p58.1" class="ltx_p">This fact follows directly from the definition of <math id="S2.p58.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{xy}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{xy}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S2.p58.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{xyz}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{xyz}.</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p59" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p59.1" class="ltx_p">By <em id="S2.p59.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Fact 3</em> and <em id="S2.p59.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Fact 4</em>, for any
two vertices <math id="S2.p59.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x,y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x,y</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.p59.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, there exist at least two vertices in <math id="S2.p59.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{xy}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{xy}</annotation></semantics></math>, or
for three vertices <math id="S2.p59.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x,y,z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x,y,z</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.p59.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, there exist at least three vertices in
<math id="S2.p59.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{xyz}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{xyz}</annotation></semantics></math>. Now, consider another pair of vertices <math id="S2.p59.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{\prime},y^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{\prime},y^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> in
<math id="S2.p59.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S-x-y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S-x-y</annotation></semantics></math> or another trio <math id="S2.p59.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{\prime},y^{\prime},z^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{\prime},y^{\prime},z^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.p59.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S-x-y-z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S-x-y-z</annotation></semantics></math>. These
choices generate at least two vertices in <math id="S2.p59.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{x^{\prime}y^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{x^{\prime}y^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> or at
least three vertices in <math id="S2.p59.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{x^{\prime}y^{\prime}z^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{x^{\prime}y^{\prime}z^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. By
<em id="S2.p59.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Fact 5</em>, repeating this process iteratively, we obtain <math id="S2.p59.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|S\right|\geq\left|U\right|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|S\right|\geq\left|U\right|</annotation></semantics></math>, which is a contradiction.
Therefore, this situation cannot arise.
 <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p60" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p60.1" class="ltx_p">The “connected” assumption in Theorem
<a href="#S2.Thmtheorem8" title="Theorem 2.8 ‣ 2 Quasi-regularizability of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs ‣ Log-concavity of the independence polynomials of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.8</span></a> is essential. For instance, the graph <math id="S2.p60.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=K_{p}\cup K_{p+2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=K_{p}\cup K_{p+2}</annotation></semantics></math> is a
<math id="S2.p60.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> graph with <math id="S2.p60.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n(G)=2p+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n(G)=2p+2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p60.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha(G)=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha(G)=2</annotation></semantics></math>, yet <math id="S2.p60.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is not
<math id="S2.p60.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>-quasi-regularizable. Moreover, in the context of Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem8" title="Theorem 2.8 ‣ 2 Quasi-regularizability of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs ‣ Log-concavity of the independence polynomials of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.8</span></a>, it is
worth noting that there exist <math id="S2.p60.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>-quasi-regularizable graphs which do not
belong to <math id="S2.p60.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>. For instance, consider the graph <math id="S2.p60.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> depicted in
Figure <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 2 Quasi-regularizability of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs ‣ Log-concavity of the independence polynomials of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. Clearly, <math id="S2.p60.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.p60.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-quasi-regularizable. However,
<math id="S2.p60.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\notin\mathbf{W}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>∉</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\notin\mathbf{W}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> because the disjoint independent sets <math id="S2.p60.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{u\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{u\}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S2.p60.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{v\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{v\}</annotation></semantics></math> cannot be extended to two disjoint maximum independent sets in <math id="S2.p60.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
