<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\cdot\alpha(H)+2\sqrt{p\cdot\alpha(H)+p}&lt;n\left(H\right)\leq\frac{\allowbreak\left(\alpha\left(H\right)^{2}+1\right)\cdot p+\left(\alpha\left(H\right)-1\right)^{2}}{\alpha\left(H\right)-1}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\cdot\alpha(H)+2\sqrt{p\cdot\alpha(H)+p}&lt;n\left(H\right)\leq\frac{\allowbreak\left(\alpha\left(H\right)^{2}+1\right)\cdot p+\left(\alpha\left(H\right)-1\right)^{2}}{\alpha\left(H\right)-1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">hold for every connected component <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(G;x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(G;x)</annotation></semantics></math> is log-concave.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p14" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof.</span> If <math id="S3.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is connected, the theorem follows from Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3.3 ‣ 3 Log-concavity of independence polynomials of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs ‣ Log-concavity of the independence polynomials of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p15.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S3.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is disconnected with <math id="S3.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(G)=q\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(G)=q\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S3.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is a disjoint union of
connected components <math id="S3.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S3.p15.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq q</annotation></semantics></math>. By Theorem
<a href="#S2.Thmtheorem6" title="Theorem 2.6 ‣ 2 Quasi-regularizability of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs ‣ Log-concavity of the independence polynomials of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.6</span></a>, <math id="S3.p15.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{i}\in\mathbf{W}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{i}\in\mathbf{W}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>. By Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3.3 ‣ 3 Log-concavity of independence polynomials of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs ‣ Log-concavity of the independence polynomials of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>,
<math id="S3.p15.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(H_{i};x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(H_{i};x)</annotation></semantics></math> is log-concave. By Lemma <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem4" title="Lemma 1.4 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Log-concavity of the independence polynomials of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.4</span></a>, we finally obtain that</p>
<table id="S3.Ex34" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex34.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="I(G;x)=I(H_{1};x)\cdot I(H_{2};x)\cdots I(H_{q};x)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>I</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>I</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>I</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(G;x)=I(H_{1};x)\cdot I(H_{2};x)\cdots I(H_{q};x)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p15.2" class="ltx_p">is log-concave as well.
 <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 3.6</span></span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> graph with <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\neq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\neq 2</annotation></semantics></math>. If
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p+1)\cdot\alpha(G)=n(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p+1)\cdot\alpha(G)=n(G)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\geq\frac{\alpha(G)^{2}}{4(\alpha(G)+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>≥</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\geq\frac{\alpha(G)^{2}}{4(\alpha(G)+1)}</annotation></semantics></math>,
then <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(G;x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(G;x)</annotation></semantics></math> is log-concave.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p16" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p16.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p16.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof.</span> Clearly, if <math id="S3.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p+1)\cdot\alpha\left(G\right)=n\left(G\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p+1)\cdot\alpha\left(G\right)=n\left(G\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, then the
same is true for every connected component <math id="S3.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, <math id="S3.p16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p+1)\cdot\alpha\left(H\right)=n\left(H\right)\leq p\cdot\alpha\left(H\right)+2\sqrt{p\cdot\alpha(H)+p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p+1)\cdot\alpha\left(H\right)=n\left(H\right)\leq p\cdot\alpha\left(H\right)+2\sqrt{p\cdot\alpha(H)+p}</annotation></semantics></math>, whenever <math id="S3.p16.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\geq\frac{\alpha(H)^{2}}{4(\alpha(H)+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>≥</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\geq\frac{\alpha(H)^{2}}{4(\alpha(H)+1)}</annotation></semantics></math>. In addition, Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem6" title="Theorem 2.6 ‣ 2 Quasi-regularizability of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs ‣ Log-concavity of the independence polynomials of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.6</span></a> claims that every
<math id="S3.p16.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\in\mathbf{W}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\in\mathbf{W}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, by Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3.3 ‣ 3 Log-concavity of independence polynomials of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs ‣ Log-concavity of the independence polynomials of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>, <math id="S3.p16.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(H;x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(H;x)</annotation></semantics></math> is
log-concave. Finally, by Lemma <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem4" title="Lemma 1.4 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Log-concavity of the independence polynomials of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.4</span></a>, <math id="S3.p16.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(G;x)={\displaystyle\prod\limits_{H}}I(H;x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mi>H</mi></munder></mstyle><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(G;x)={\displaystyle\prod\limits_{H}}I(H;x)</annotation></semantics></math> is log-concave.
 <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p17.1" class="ltx_p">The disjoint union of two graphs <math id="S3.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is the graph <math id="S3.p17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=G_{1}\cup G_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=G_{1}\cup G_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> whose vertex set is the disjoint union of <math id="S3.p17.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(G_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(G_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p17.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(G_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(G_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>,
and whose edge set is the disjoint union of <math id="S3.p17.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(G_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(G_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p17.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(G_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(G_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Specifically, <math id="S3.p17.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="qG" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">qG</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the disjoint union of <math id="S3.p17.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> copies of the graph
<math id="S3.p17.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. The join (or Zykov sum) of <math id="S3.p17.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p17.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is the graph <math id="S3.p17.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{1}+G_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{1}+G_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, with vertex set <math id="S3.p17.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(G_{1})\cup V(G_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(G_{1})\cup V(G_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> and edge set <math id="S3.p17.m15" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="E(G_{1})\cup E(G_{2})\cup\{v_{1}v_{2}:v_{1}\in V(G_{1}),v_{2}\in V(G_{2})\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(G_{1})\cup E(G_{2})\cup\{v_{1}v_{2}:v_{1}\in V(G_{1}),v_{2}\in V(G_{2})\}</annotation></semantics></math>. The
lexicographic product <math id="S3.p17.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G[H]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G[H]</annotation></semantics></math> of the graphs <math id="S3.p17.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p17.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as follows:
<math id="S3.p17.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\left(G[H]\right)=V\left(G\right)\times V\left(H\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\left(G[H]\right)=V\left(G\right)\times V\left(H\right)</annotation></semantics></math> and two
verices <math id="S3.p17.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g_{1},h_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g_{1},h_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p17.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g_{2},h_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g_{2},h_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> are adjacent in <math id="S3.p17.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G[H]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G[H]</annotation></semantics></math> if and only
if either <math id="S3.p17.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{1}g_{2}\in E\left(G\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{1}g_{2}\in E\left(G\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, or <math id="S3.p17.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{1}=g_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{1}=g_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S3.p17.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{1}h_{2}\in E\left(H\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{1}h_{2}\in E\left(H\right)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 3.7</span></span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="24\leq n\leq 2452" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>24</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>2452</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">24\leq n\leq 2452</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=K_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=K_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H=4K_{10}+K_{n(4)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>10</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H=4K_{10}+K_{n(4)}</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{n(4)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{n(4)}</annotation></semantics></math> is the complete <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-partite graph where each of the <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> parts
has <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> vertices. Then <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> graph, while <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is a
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> graph <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, p. 240]</cite>. By <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>, Theorem 8.2]</cite>, the
lexicographic product <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G[H]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G[H]</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> graph. The
independence polynomials of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> are as follows:</span></p>
