<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Notice that this polynomial is not log-concave.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p18.1" class="ltx_p">A well-covered graph <math id="S3.p18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> having no isolated vertices and satisfying
<math id="S3.p18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\cdot\alpha(G)=n(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\cdot\alpha(G)=n(G)</annotation></semantics></math> is called <span id="S3.p18.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">very well-covered</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>. Previously, it was established that for any integer <math id="S3.p18.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\geq 8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\geq 8</annotation></semantics></math>, there
exist connected well-covered graphs <math id="S3.p18.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.p18.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\left(G\right)=\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\left(G\right)=\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, whose independence polynomials are not unimodal (hence, not log-concave)
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>. It was also shown that the independence polynomial of a
very well-covered graph <math id="S3.p18.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is unimodal when <math id="S3.p18.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\left(G\right)\leq 9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\left(G\right)\leq 9</annotation></semantics></math>
and is log-concave when <math id="S3.p18.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\left(G\right)\leq 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\left(G\right)\leq 5</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>. Now,
Corollary <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem6" title="Corollary 3.6 ‣ 3 Log-concavity of independence polynomials of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs ‣ Log-concavity of the independence polynomials of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.6</span></a> corroborates <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, Theorem 2.4(v)]</cite> and
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, Conjecture 3.10]</cite> for the case <math id="S3.p18.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha(G)\leq 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha(G)\leq 5</annotation></semantics></math>, as well.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 3.8</span></span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a very well-covered graph. If <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha(G)\leq 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha(G)\leq 5</annotation></semantics></math>, then
the independence polynomial of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is log-concave.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p19" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p19.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p19.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof.</span> Let <math id="S3.p19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\alpha(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\alpha(G)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(G;x)=\sum_{k=0}^{\alpha}s_{k}x^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>α</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(G;x)=\sum_{k=0}^{\alpha}s_{k}x^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the
independence number and independence polynomial of <math id="S3.p19.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. Recall
that a very well-covered graph is a well-covered graph with order <math id="S3.p19.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>.
To prove that <math id="S3.p19.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(G;x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(G;x)</annotation></semantics></math> is log-concave, based on the proof of Theorem
<a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3.3 ‣ 3 Log-concavity of independence polynomials of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs ‣ Log-concavity of the independence polynomials of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>, it suffices to verify that the inequality</p>
<table id="S3.Ex39" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{2}-\alpha\cdot k+\alpha+1\geq 0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{2}-\alpha\cdot k+\alpha+1\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p19.2" class="ltx_p">holds for all <math id="S3.p19.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq k\leq\alpha-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq k\leq\alpha-1</annotation></semantics></math>. This condition is equivalent to requiring
<math id="S3.p19.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\leq 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\leq 5</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, the independence polynomial of <math id="S3.p19.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is log-concave
for all <math id="S3.p19.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\leq 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\leq 5</annotation></semantics></math>.
 <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.9</span></span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, Corollary 2.3]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>, Theorem 4]</cite>) Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> be a graph. Then <math id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\circ K_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\circ K_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> graph, but it is not a
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{p+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{p+1}</annotation></semantics></math> graph.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p20" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p20.1" class="ltx_p">Consequently, this leads to the following.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 3.10</span></span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> be a graph of order <math id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. The polynomial <math id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(H\circ K_{p};x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(H\circ K_{p};x)</annotation></semantics></math> is log-concave for every <math id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\geq\frac{n^{2}}{4(n+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>≥</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\geq\frac{n^{2}}{4(n+1)}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p21" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p21.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p21.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof.</span> Taking into account that <math id="S3.p21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n(H\circ K_{p})=(p+1)\cdot n\left(H\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n(H\circ K_{p})=(p+1)\cdot n\left(H\right)</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S3.p21.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha(H\circ K_{p})=n\left(H\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha(H\circ K_{p})=n\left(H\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, we obtain that</p>
<table id="S3.Ex40" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex40.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n(H\circ K_{p})=(p+1)\cdot\alpha(H\circ K_{p})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n(H\circ K_{p})=(p+1)\cdot\alpha(H\circ K_{p}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p21.2" class="ltx_p">Consequently, <math id="S3.p21.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(H\circ K_{p};x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(H\circ K_{p};x)</annotation></semantics></math> is log-concave for all <math id="S3.p21.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, in
accordance with Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem9" title="Lemma 3.9 ‣ 3 Log-concavity of independence polynomials of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs ‣ Log-concavity of the independence polynomials of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.9</span></a> and Corollary <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem6" title="Corollary 3.6 ‣ 3 Log-concavity of independence polynomials of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs ‣ Log-concavity of the independence polynomials of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.6</span></a>.
 <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 3.11</span></span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">[</span><a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a><span id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">]</span></cite><span id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\circ\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\circ\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> be a clique corona graph, where
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}=\{K_{p\left(v\right)}:v\in V(G)\text{ and }p\left(v\right)\geq 1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}=\{K_{p\left(v\right)}:v\in V(G)\text{ and }p\left(v\right)\geq 1\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=\min\limits_{v\in V(G)}p\left(v\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=\min\limits_{v\in V(G)}p\left(v\right)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\circ\mathcal{H}\in\mathbf{W}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\circ\mathcal{H}\in\mathbf{W}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem12" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 3.12</span></span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=\min\limits_{v\in V(G)}p\left(v\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=\min\limits_{v\in V(G)}p\left(v\right)</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}=\{K_{p\left(v\right)}:v\in V(G)\text{ and }p\left(v\right)\geq 1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}=\{K_{p\left(v\right)}:v\in V(G)\text{ and }p\left(v\right)\geq 1\}</annotation></semantics></math>. The polynomial <math id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(G\circ\mathcal{H};x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(G\circ\mathcal{H};x)</annotation></semantics></math> is log-concave for
all <math id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\geq\frac{n(G)^{2}}{4(n(G)+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>≥</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\geq\frac{n(G)^{2}}{4(n(G)+1)}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p22" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p22.1" class="ltx_p">As a consequence, the following corollary partially confirms that the
conjecture stated in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, Conjecture 4.2]</cite> also holds true.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem13" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 3.13</span></span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is a well-covered tree with at least two vertices with
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha(G)\leq 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha(G)\leq 5</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(G;x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(G;x)</annotation></semantics></math> is log-concave.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p23" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p23.1" class="ltx_p">It is established that taking the corona of any graph <math id="S3.p23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.p23.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> yields
the very well-covered graph <math id="S3.p23.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\circ K_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\circ K_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>, Corollary 3]</cite>). By
applying this method, we can generate an infinite family of very well-covered
trees based on any given tree. This brings us to the following.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem14" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 3.14</span></span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem14.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem14.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem14.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any tree <math id="S3.Thmtheorem14.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.Thmtheorem14.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha(T)\leq 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha(T)\leq 5</annotation></semantics></math>, the independence polynomials of the
following graphs</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex41" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex41.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\circ K_{1},\left(T\circ K_{1}\right)\circ K_{1},\left(\left(T\circ K_{1}\right)\circ K_{1}\right)\circ K_{1},\left(\left(\left(T\circ K_{1}\right)\circ K_{1}\right)\circ K_{1}\right)\circ K_{1},\ldots" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\circ K_{1},\left(T\circ K_{1}\right)\circ K_{1},\left(\left(T\circ K_{1}\right)\circ K_{1}\right)\circ K_{1},\left(\left(\left(T\circ K_{1}\right)\circ K_{1}\right)\circ K_{1}\right)\circ K_{1},\ldots</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem14.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem14.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">are log-concave.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This paper primarily addresses problems related to log-concavity
of <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> graphs. Our results indicate that the independence
polynomial <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(G;x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(G;x)</annotation></semantics></math> is log-concave whenever <math id="S4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> is sufficiently large
relative to the independence number <math id="S4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha(G)</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore, Zhu, in
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>, Corollary 3.3]</cite>, demonstrated the log-concave preservation of the
independence polynomial of <math id="S4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\circ K_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\circ K_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> whenever the independence polynomial
of <math id="S4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is log-concave. Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 3.5 ‣ 3 Log-concavity of independence polynomials of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs ‣ Log-concavity of the independence polynomials of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a> leads us to the following.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prob">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem 4.1</span></span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">What conditions on the <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> graph <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> guarantee that the
independence polynomial <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(G;x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(G;x)</annotation></semantics></math> is log-concave or at least unimodal?</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In addition, Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem8" title="Theorem 2.8 ‣ 2 Quasi-regularizability of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs ‣ Log-concavity of the independence polynomials of 𝐖_𝑝 graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.8</span></a> motivates the following.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_conjecture">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conjecture 4.2</span></span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a connected <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> graph. Then <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-quasi-regularizable if and only if <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n(G)\geq 3\cdot\alpha(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n(G)\geq 3\cdot\alpha(G)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
